// ADHD Reflect — Standalone Topic Guides
// 20 research-backed guides organized by topic

export const TOPIC_GUIDES = [
  {
    id: "g01",
    title: `Your nervous system under pressure`,
    topic: "nervous-system",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "escaper"],
    perspectives: ["me"],
    recognize: `You went from fine to furious in what felt like one second. Or you felt the heat building but could not stop the reaction. Or your body did something, yelled, snapped, threw something, before your brain had decided to do it.`,
    brain: `Your ADHD brain has a shorter gap between trigger and reaction than a neurotypical brain. This is not a metaphor. The prefrontal cortex, the part of the brain responsible for impulse control, emotional regulation, and pause-before-you-act, is the exact region most affected by ADHD. It develops later, works less consistently, and goes offline faster under stress.

When load builds, noise, time pressure, repeated demands, accumulated frustration, your nervous system shifts into a threat-reduction mode. The amygdala, your brain's alarm system, fires before the prefrontal cortex can intervene. The reaction is not the problem. The missing pause is.

This is why you can be patient for an hour and then lose it over the smallest thing. The small thing was not the trigger. It was the last input in a sequence that your regulatory system could no longer hold. Your nervous system did not fail at that moment. It ran out of runway.

Cortisol and adrenaline are real chemicals doing real things in your body during these moments. The heat, the tightness, the sharpening of your voice, these are physiological responses, not character flaws. You cannot think your way out of an activated nervous system. You have to move through it.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child's nervous system mirrors yours, literally. Limbic resonance means a child's brain entrains to the emotional state of the nearby adult. When you escalate, their amygdala reads your state as danger and amplifies their own response.

If your child also has ADHD, they have the same shortened prefrontal gap. Two reactive nervous systems in the same room will almost always amplify each other. They are not choosing to escalate. Their brain is mirroring yours.

By age 12, a child with ADHD has received approximately 20,000 more corrective or negative messages than their neurotypical peers. Each new sharp tone adds to that accumulated weight. Their reaction to your reaction is not just about this moment. It is about every moment that came before it.`,
    dualDynamic: `This is the central operating problem of dual-ADHD households. Two nervous systems with shortened regulatory runways, sharing a physical space, each capable of triggering the other. The standard parenting advice, stay calm, be consistent, use a measured tone, was designed for brains that have longer pauses between feeling and action. It is not that you are not trying hard enough. The tools were not built for your neurology.

The most reliable pattern interrupt is environmental, not cognitive. You cannot think your way to calm when your prefrontal cortex is offline. You can change the physical environment: leave the room, lower the lights, reduce the noise, create physical distance. The environment changes what your nervous system is processing, which changes what it does.`,
    strategies: [`Reduce the input before you correct the behaviour. Turn something off, move to a quieter space, use fewer words. Lower what your nervous system is processing before you add more to it.`, `Learn your body's early warning signal. For most people it is a specific physical sensation: heat in the chest, tightening of the jaw, a change in breathing. When you notice it, that is your exit cue, not the beginning of trying harder.`, `Exit before explosion, not after. The words 'I need one minute' are a complete sentence. Leave the room. Thirty seconds of separation prevents thirty minutes of damage.`, `Cold water on your wrists or face activates the parasympathetic nervous system, your body's off-switch for the threat response. This is not a lifestyle tip. It is a physiological intervention.`, `After the moment, do not immediately teach or lecture. Regulation first, relationship second, reason last. This is the order the brain can receive input.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When you feel the heat rising`, say: `I need one minute. I am coming back.` },
      { situation: `When you have already raised your voice`, say: `That was too loud. I am going to step away and come back quieter.` },
      { situation: `After the moment has passed`, say: `I lost it and I should not have. You did not deserve that volume.` },
      { situation: `When your child escalates in response`, say: `We are both getting big right now. Let us take a break and come back to this.` },
    ],
    partner: `They see the reaction. They do not see the thirty minutes of accumulated load that preceded it. They may read your snap as disproportionate or unpredictable because from their vantage point, everything was fine and then suddenly it was not. What they missed: the noise that had been too loud for twenty minutes, the instruction repeated four times, the thing at work that had not resolved. They see the match. They do not see what was already burning. If they also have ADHD, they may be running their own reactive pattern simultaneously, which doubles the volatility in the room.`,
    moreHelp: `If you are losing it most days, if the reactions are getting bigger rather than smaller, if you are frightened of what you might do, or if your child is showing signs of being afraid of you, talk to someone. A GP, an ADHD-aware therapist, or a parenting coach who understands ADHD can help you build the environmental and pharmacological supports that reduce the frequency and intensity. This is not about willpower. It is about getting the right support for your specific neurology.`,
    relatedCards: ["m07", "m08", "m09", "m01", "m02", "m06", "p01", "p09"],
  },
  {
    id: "g02",
    title: `Working memory and the invisible load`,
    topic: "executive-function",
    patternTypes: ["juggler"],
    perspectives: ["me"],
    recognize: `You forgot the form. You missed the appointment. You promised something and it fell out of your head before you could act on it. The systems you built last month have already collapsed. You are always rebuilding, never maintaining.`,
    brain: `ADHD working memory holds fewer items for a shorter duration than neurotypical working memory. This is one of the most consistently documented ADHD impairments across hundreds of studies. It is not about intelligence, effort, or caring. It is a capacity limitation.

Parenting has an enormous invisible overhead. Dates, commitments, routines, consequences, relationship debts, logistics, and transitions, most of which do not exist in a visible system. They live in working memory, which is exactly where ADHD is weakest.

The collapse is not a character flaw. It is a structural mismatch between what parenting requires and what the ADHD executive function system reliably provides. You are not rebuilding systems because you are lazy. You are rebuilding them because you need external structures to hold what a neurotypical brain might hold internally, and keeping those structures maintained is itself a task that requires working memory.

The cruel irony: the things that matter most to you, your child's feelings, their important events, the promises you made, are the things that cause the most pain when working memory drops them. You did not forget because you did not care. You forgot because the brain that cares deeply is the same brain that cannot hold the reminder.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child's working memory works the same way. The instruction that entered their ears did not make it to their hands. The homework they were told about at school dropped out before they reached their bag. The lunch box they were reminded about three times still sits on the bench.

They are not ignoring you. They are losing the instruction between hearing and doing. The gap between those two things is where ADHD lives. Each forgotten item, each missed step, each 'I told you three times' adds another layer of shame to a child who is already carrying more negative messages than their peers.`,
    dualDynamic: `Two people with impaired working memory trying to run a household is a systems problem, not a willpower problem. The parent forgets the form. The child forgets the lunch box. The other parent, if they also have ADHD, forgets that someone was supposed to remind someone else. The failure cascades.

The solution is never 'try harder to remember.' The solution is always 'build an external system that does the remembering.' Every working memory demand you remove from the human and place on the system is one fewer failure, one fewer disappointment, one fewer dose of shame.`,
    strategies: [`One external anchor for one thing is more reliable than any promise to remember. Write it where you will see it, not on your phone, physically visible in the space where you will need it.`, `Photo the form the second it comes home. It now exists in two places. The paper can be lost. The photo cannot.`, `Set calendar alerts with a seven-day lead time for recurring tasks like medication refills, school fees, and permission slips. The alert does the remembering so your brain does not have to.`, `Reduce the number of systems. Three apps, a whiteboard, a calendar, and a notebook is five systems that each need maintaining. One system used consistently beats five used intermittently.`, `When you forget something that mattered to your child, name it directly: 'I missed that. I am going to write it down right now so it does not happen again.' Not self-punishment. One concrete action, immediately.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When you forgot something important`, say: `I forgot and I am sorry. That was not okay. I am putting a reminder in my phone right now so it does not happen again.` },
      { situation: `When your child forgot something`, say: `Where can we put this so your eyes will see it when you need it? Not in your head. Somewhere real.` },
      { situation: `When a system collapses`, say: `That system stopped working. What is the smallest version of it that could work? Let us try that.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may experience your forgotten commitments as not caring, or your pattern of starting and not finishing as unreliability. They probably pick up the tracking they can see has been dropped, the forms, the appointments, the follow-through, and this creates an invisible load imbalance they may be quietly resentful about. What they need to know: the cognitive effort you are expending just to stay at the surface is enormous. The tracking you are doing is invisible. The shame that follows each collapse is real. If they can take ownership of one recurring task permanently, that is one fewer failure point for the whole family.`,
    moreHelp: `If the forgotten things are causing serious consequences, missed medical appointments, unpaid bills, repeated school problems, consider ADHD coaching. A coach helps you build external systems tailored to your specific failure patterns. This is not therapy. It is practical scaffolding for executive function. Medication also significantly improves working memory for many people. If you are unmedicated or under-medicated, talk to your prescriber about whether your current treatment is addressing the executive function components.`,
    relatedCards: ["p14", "p15", "p16", "p17", "p24", "p42", "p35", "k01", "k02", "m18"],
  },
  {
    id: "g03",
    title: `The shame spiral after you lose it`,
    topic: "emotional-regulation",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller"],
    perspectives: ["me"],
    recognize: `You lost it. And then you spent the next two hours replaying exactly what you said and how their face looked. The shame is consuming the energy you need for the repair. You cannot stop the loop.`,
    brain: `ADHD is linked to heightened rejection sensitivity and a longer emotional hangover after distressing events. The emotions take longer to return to baseline than they do for neurotypical adults. The shame spiral is not self-indulgence. It is your nervous system staying activated long after the triggering event has passed.

The spiral serves a function. At some level, the rumination feels like accountability. As though if you think about it for long enough and feel bad enough about it, you will have paid for it and it will not happen again. But that is not how behaviour change works. The energy going into the spiral is energy not going into the repair, and it is the repair that actually changes the relationship.

The neurological mechanism is specific. ADHD brains have fewer dopamine transporters in regions involved in reward processing and emotional regulation. The negative emotional signal gets amplified and sustained. The 'off switch' for the bad feeling does not work as reliably as it does in neurotypical brains. You are not choosing to ruminate. Your brain is stuck in a processing loop it cannot exit efficiently.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child sees the blow-up. Then they see the tense silence while you are inside the spiral. Then possibly your over-reach for warmth or reassurance. What they are less likely to see is a clean, simple repair.

Children are not fragile. They can recover from hard moments when the repair is consistent. What they find harder is the unpredictability of the cycle: intensity, then withdrawal, then over-warmth, then the next intensity. The spiral makes that cycle less visible to you and harder to break.

If your child also has ADHD, they may be running their own shame spiral about their role in the conflict. Two shame spirals in one house, neither visible to the other.`,
    dualDynamic: `The shame spiral often prevents the repair, which is the one thing that would actually help both of you. The parent is too deep in self-recrimination to approach the child calmly. The child is left in the aftermath without closure.

The repair does not need to be large. Two sentences, delivered when both of you are calm. One sentence that names what happened. One that names what you are going to try. Then stop. Do not wait for absolution. Do not ask if they are okay with it. Two sentences and a return to normal. The repair is in the return to normal, not in the speech.`,
    strategies: [`Set a time limit on the spiral. Tell yourself: I am allowed to feel bad about this for 30 minutes. After that, I do the repair. The time limit is artificial but it works because it gives the rumination a boundary.`, `The two-sentence repair: one sentence that names what happened, one that names what you will try. Say it when both of you are calm. Not an extended apology. Not a promise that might not hold.`, `After the repair, do something ordinary together. Sit with them. Get them a snack. Help with something. The return to normal is the repair, not the words.`, `Write it down once, briefly, and close the notebook. The act of writing can break the rumination loop because it externalises the thought.`, `Notice if the spiral is about the child or about your identity as a parent. 'I yelled and I should not have' is workable. 'I am a terrible parent' is shame talking. The first leads to repair. The second leads to paralysis.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `The two-sentence repair`, say: `I yelled this morning and I should not have. I am going to work on pausing before I get to that point.` },
      { situation: `When you are over-apologising`, say: `I am sorry about earlier. I do not need you to make me feel better about it. I just wanted you to know I know.` },
      { situation: `When the spiral starts`, say: `I am noticing the spiral. It is trying to make me feel worse than the situation deserves. I am going to do the repair instead.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may see what looks like moving on too quickly, or excessive apology, or warmth that seems disproportionate to what just happened. They may not understand why, three days later, you are still quietly subdued about a moment that from their view has been addressed. What they need to know: the private inventory is running in the background. The amount of cognitive space the spiral occupies is significant. They may have thought you were fine because you functioned. But you were running with a large background load.`,
    moreHelp: `If the shame spiral lasts days rather than hours, if it is preventing you from repairing, if you are avoiding your child because the guilt is too big, or if the self-talk has moved from 'I did a bad thing' to 'I am a bad person,' talk to a therapist. ADHD-aware cognitive behavioural therapy can specifically target the rumination pattern. Rejection sensitive dysphoria, if severe, sometimes responds to medication adjustments.`,
    relatedCards: ["p01", "p02", "p03", "m15", "p09", "m17", "p19"],
  },
  {
    id: "g04",
    title: `Two nervous systems in one room`,
    topic: "dual-adhd",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "looper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `Your child melted down and you joined them. Or you started calm and within sixty seconds you were both yelling. Or you watched yourself escalate knowing you should stop and could not.`,
    brain: `Limbic resonance is the neurological mechanism by which one person's emotional state entrains the emotional state of another person nearby. When your child is dysregulated, your amygdala picks up their distress signals, tone of voice, facial expression, body tension, and begins matching them.

In a neurotypical parent, the prefrontal cortex moderates this resonance. It says: they are upset, I can stay calm. In an ADHD parent, that moderation is less reliable. The resonance entrains your nervous system faster than your regulatory system can intervene.

The result: two dysregulated people in one room, each amplifying the other. Your volume increases their volume. Their refusal increases your frustration. Your frustration increases their shutdown. The cycle runs because neither brain has the regulatory capacity to break it from the inside.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child cannot regulate if you cannot regulate. Co-regulation, the process by which a child borrows a parent's calm nervous system to settle their own, requires you to be calm first. If your nervous system is also in alarm, there is nothing to borrow.

This is not a moral failing. It is a structural limitation of the dual-ADHD household. One of you has to go first, and it has to be you, because you are the adult. But your brain makes that harder than it sounds.`,
    dualDynamic: `The standard advice, stay calm and your child will calm down, is neurologically accurate but practically insufficient for ADHD parents. Your calm is less reliable, less accessible under load, and depletes faster. You need a different strategy.

The strategy that works: environmental intervention. You cannot regulate your way to calm through willpower alone. But you can change the physical environment, and the environment changes what your nervous system is processing.

Leave the room. Lower the lights. Turn off the sound. Reduce the number of people in the space. Create physical distance. Each environmental change reduces the sensory input your nervous system is processing, which gives your prefrontal cortex a chance to come back online.`,
    strategies: [`Physical distance is the most reliable pattern interrupt. You do not need to leave the house. You need three metres and thirty seconds.`, `Lower your voice below the level you think is necessary. Your child's nervous system calibrates to your volume. Quieter from you means quieter from them, with a thirty-second delay.`, `One of you has to go first. It will not be the child. Accept this and stop waiting for them to calm down before you do.`, `After the co-escalation, do not immediately process it. Both nervous systems need to return to baseline before any conversation about what happened. This takes a minimum of 20 minutes for most people.`, `Name the pattern out loud when you are both calm: 'We got big together. That happens when both our brains are tired. Next time I am going to try to leave the room before we get to that point.'`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `Mid-escalation`, say: `We are both getting too big. I am going to step out for one minute.` },
      { situation: `When they chase you as you exit`, say: `I am not leaving you. I am coming back. I need my brain to get quieter first.` },
      { situation: `After both are calm`, say: `Our brains did the thing again where they set each other off. That is not your fault and it is not mine. It is what ADHD brains do when they are tired.` },
    ],
    partner: `If your partner witnesses a co-escalation, they may intervene in a way that makes you feel judged. Or they may take over in a way that implies you have failed. What they need to understand: the co-escalation is a neurological event, not a parenting failure. The most helpful thing they can do is take the child or take you out of the room without commentary. Debrief later, privately, not in front of the child.`,
    moreHelp: `If co-escalations are happening daily, if they are becoming physically intense, or if you or your child are being hurt, seek professional support immediately. A family therapist who understands ADHD can teach co-regulation strategies specific to dual-ADHD households. Parental medication optimisation can also significantly extend the regulatory runway.`,
    relatedCards: ["m07", "m08", "m12", "m03", "p06", "p21"],
  },
  {
    id: "g05",
    title: `Rejection sensitivity in parent and child`,
    topic: "emotional-regulation",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller", "looper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `A mild comment devastated your child. Or a relative's remark about your parenting cut deeper than it should have. Or your partner said something reasonable and your body reacted as though they had attacked you.`,
    brain: `Rejection Sensitive Dysphoria is increasingly recognised as a core ADHD feature. The neural circuits that process social rejection overlap with physical pain circuits. A mild correction, an unanswered text, a perceived slight, these activate the same brain regions as being physically hurt.

This is not sensitivity as a personality trait. It is neural processing. The emotional response arrives at full intensity with no dimmer switch. The reasonable cognitive understanding that 'they probably did not mean it that way' cannot reach the processing centre fast enough to moderate the pain.

For ADHD parents, RSD creates a specific vulnerability. You are more sensitive to perceived judgment about your parenting, which is the area where you already feel most inadequate. A teacher's concern, a relative's comment, a partner's suggestion, these can all activate the RSD circuit and produce a defensive response that is disproportionate to the actual input.

The pain is real. The interpretation may not be accurate. Both things are true at the same time.`,
    kidBrain: `By age 12, a child with ADHD has received approximately 20,000 more corrective or negative messages than their neurotypical peers. Each correction, each 'stop that,' each disappointed face adds to an accumulated weight of evidence that they are not good enough.

When your child falls apart over a small criticism, they are not reacting to this criticism. They are reacting to this criticism plus every one that came before it. The pile is heavy.

Your response to their failure matters more than the failure itself. 'You made a mistake and mistakes are fixable' builds a different neural pathway than 'try harder next time.' They need to learn that failure is an event, not an identity.`,
    dualDynamic: `An ADHD parent with RSD correcting an ADHD child with RSD is a minefield. Your correction, however gentle, activates their rejection circuit. Their response, shutdown, tears, defiance, activates your rejection circuit. You feel rejected by their rejection of your correction.

The way through: deliver corrections in the lowest emotional temperature possible. Brief. Specific. Without disappointment in your voice. And separate the correction from the relationship: 'I love you and this specific thing needs to change' not 'I am disappointed in you.'`,
    strategies: [`Validate the feeling before reality-testing the interpretation. 'That sounds like it really hurt' before 'What else could they have meant?' The validation must come first or the reframe is heard as dismissal.`, `Notice when your RSD is responding to your child's RSD. The loop is: you correct, they collapse, you feel guilty, you over-correct, they are confused. Name it.`, `For your own RSD: pause before responding to perceived criticism about your parenting. The first interpretation your brain offers is usually the most painful one. Give yourself 60 seconds to find a second interpretation.`, `For your child's RSD: reduce the volume of corrections. Pick the three that matter today and let the rest go. Fewer corrections means less RSD activation, which means better reception of the corrections you do give.`, `Build a 'rejection audit' practice. After an RSD spike, ask yourself: what did they actually say? What did my brain tell me they said? These are usually different.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When your child is devastated by feedback`, say: `That sounds like it really hurt. I can see it is big for you right now.` },
      { situation: `After the RSD wave has passed`, say: `Your brain made that feel like the worst thing. What actually happened?` },
      { situation: `When a relative triggers your RSD`, say: `I hear the concern. We are handling it.` },
      { situation: `When correcting your child`, say: `This one thing needs to change. Everything else is fine. You are fine.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may not understand why a small comment produced such a large reaction, in you or your child. What they need to know: RSD means the emotional processing of social input is amplified. The comment that felt small to them landed at full volume. The most helpful thing: 'I can see that landed hard. I did not mean it the way it sounded.' Simple. Acknowledging. Not dismissive.`,
    moreHelp: `If RSD is significantly impacting daily functioning, relationships, or your child's willingness to try new things, discuss it with your prescriber. Some ADHD medications, particularly alpha-2 agonists, have shown benefit for emotional dysregulation including RSD. Cognitive behavioural therapy can also help build the pause between the RSD spike and the behavioural response.`,
    relatedCards: ["m35", "m36", "k07", "k15", "m19", "p27", "p32", "m17"],
  },

  {
    id: "g06",
    title: `Time blindness and lateness`,
    topic: "executive-function",
    patternTypes: ["juggler"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `You are late again. They are late again. The morning started fine and somehow you are still ten minutes behind. Time passed and neither of you felt it.`,
    brain: `Time blindness is one of the most consistent ADHD impairments. The internal clock that neurotypical brains use to estimate duration, pace activity, and feel urgency does not work reliably in ADHD brains. Five minutes feels like two. Thirty minutes feels like ten. The passage of time is not experienced internally, which means deadlines, transitions, and urgency all feel abstract until the crisis point.

This is not laziness or disrespect. It is a genuine perceptual deficit. Telling someone with time blindness to hurry up is like telling someone without glasses to read smaller print. The information is not reaching the processing system.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child genuinely cannot feel time passing. The instruction 'five more minutes' is meaningless to a brain that does not experience duration. They are not defying the clock. They do not feel it. This is why they are shocked when time is up, why they underestimate how long homework will take, and why they are always surprised by lateness.`,
    dualDynamic: `Two time-blind people trying to leave the house on time is a structural impossibility without external tools. Neither of you feels the minutes passing. Neither of you naturally paces activity against a deadline. The only thing that works is making time visible: clocks in every room, visual timers, phone alarms at transition points.`,
    strategies: [`Visual timers in the rooms where transitions happen. Not phone timers, physical ones where time is visible as a shrinking quantity.`, `Build in buffer time that you both pretend does not exist. If you need to leave at 8:00, the house operates on 7:45.`, `Reduce morning decisions to zero. Clothes laid out. Bags packed. Lunch made. Everything that can be done the night before, is.`, `Use a single alarm tone that means 'we leave in five minutes.' Train the whole house to respond to it.`, `Stop saying 'hurry up.' It adds pressure without adding speed. Walk to them, make eye contact, give one concrete action.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When you are both running late`, say: `We are behind. Shoes. Now. Car in two minutes.` },
      { situation: `When your child has no sense of urgency`, say: `The timer says three minutes. When it is done, we go. Watch it with me.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may interpret your lateness as disrespect for their time. What they need to know: you are not choosing to be late. You genuinely did not feel the minutes passing. The fix is external systems, not apologies and promises to try harder.`,
    moreHelp: `If lateness is causing significant consequences at work, school, or in relationships, ADHD coaching can help build time management systems specific to your brain. Medication often improves time perception measurably.`,
    relatedCards: ["m24", "m01", "m41", "p15", "p42"],
  },

  {
    id: "g07",
    title: `Medication, rebound, and the daily cycle`,
    topic: "medication",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "juggler"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `The medication wears off and everything changes. The regulated child from school is gone and in their place is someone weepy, raging, or feral with hunger. Or your own medication wore off and the patient parent from 3pm is nowhere to be found at 5pm.`,
    brain: `Stimulant medication works by increasing dopamine and norepinephrine availability in the prefrontal cortex. When the dose wears off, these neurotransmitters drop, sometimes below the pre-medication baseline temporarily. This rebound dip is pharmacological, not behavioural.

The rebound window typically lasts 30 to 60 minutes. During this time, emotional regulation, impulse control, and frustration tolerance may be worse than if the person were completely unmedicated. The medication giveth and the medication taketh away.

Appetite suppression is the most common stimulant side effect. The hunger signal is chemically overridden during the active dose. When it returns, it arrives at full intensity. The child who ate nothing all day is now ravenous and dysregulated at the same time.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child does not understand the rebound. They were fine and now they are not, and the shift feels scary and confusing. They did not choose to crash. Their brain lost the chemical support it had all day. Telling them to calm down during a rebound is asking them to regulate with even fewer resources than usual.`,
    dualDynamic: `If parent and child are on different medication schedules, their rebound windows may overlap, creating a house with zero regulation for 30 to 60 minutes. If you can, stagger the timing so at least one regulated adult is available during the child's rebound. Talk to both prescribers about extended-release options or afternoon boosters.`,
    strategies: [`Know the rebound window and plan around it. Lower demands, higher snacks, minimal transitions during that 30 to 60 minutes.`, `Large protein-heavy breakfast before the medication kicks in. Protein-dense snack ready for when it wears off.`, `Do not try to do homework, chores, or have important conversations during the rebound. Wait 60 minutes.`, `Track: time of dose, time of crash, mood, appetite, sleep. This data helps the prescriber adjust. You cannot optimise what you do not measure.`, `If your own medication wears off before theirs, talk to your prescriber about extending your coverage through the parenting hours.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `During the child's rebound`, say: `Your brain is adjusting right now. Let us just be quiet for a bit. Snack?` },
      { situation: `When wondering about the dose`, say: `I am going to write down what I noticed today and talk to the doctor about it.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may not understand the pharmacological cycle. What they need to know: the 5pm crash is predictable and temporary. It is not the child's real personality. It is a medication transition. The household needs to plan around it the way you plan around a toddler's nap schedule.`,
    moreHelp: `If the rebound is severe, if appetite suppression is affecting growth, or if sleep is consistently disrupted by the medication, these are prescriber conversations. Medication adjustment is normal and expected. The first dose and timing are rarely the final answer.`,
    relatedCards: ["m22", "m23", "m46", "p18", "p23", "p24", "k12"],
  },

  {
    id: "g08",
    title: `Sensory overload at home`,
    topic: "sensory",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "escaper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `The noise is too much. The touching is too much. The mess is too much. Your child needs stimulation and you need silence and you share the same four walls.`,
    brain: `ADHD sensory processing is different, not defective, but different. Some ADHD brains are hypersensitive to sensory input: sounds are louder, textures are more intense, visual clutter is more distracting. Other ADHD brains are hyposensitive: they seek more input, more movement, more noise, more touch.

In a dual-ADHD household, you may have opposite sensory profiles. The parent who needs quiet and the child who needs noise. The parent who is touched out and the child who craves physical contact. These are not preferences. They are nervous system requirements.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child's sensory needs are real. The sock seam that you cannot feel is the loudest signal in their body. The food texture that seems fine to you triggers a genuine alarm response in their nervous system. Their need for movement, noise, and touch is their body generating the stimulation their brain requires to function. Asking for stillness and silence is asking them to suppress a regulation tool.`,
    dualDynamic: `The parent needs less input. The child needs more input. Both needs are valid. Both are neurological. The solution is not suppression of one, it is accommodation of both. Designated loud spaces and quiet spaces. Noise-reducing earplugs that lower volume without blocking speech. Separate rooms when both needs are at their peak. The goal is coexistence, not control.`,
    strategies: [`Noise-reducing earplugs for the parent. They lower the volume without blocking conversation. This is the single most effective sensory accommodation for ADHD parents.`, `Designate a loud space and a quiet space. The child can be loud in one, the parent can be quiet in the other.`, `Accommodate sensory needs before they become sensory crises. Cut the tags, buy the seamless socks, find the toothpaste that works.`, `When you are touched out, name it kindly: 'My body needs a break from touching. I love you and I need five minutes.'`, `Give the child alternative sensory input: a wobble cushion, a fidget tool, something to chew, weighted blanket. Meet the need through a different channel.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When the noise is too much`, say: `I need five minutes of quiet. My ears are full. I am not angry, I am full.` },
      { situation: `When they need sensory input you cannot provide`, say: `I can see your body needs to move. Go run two laps of the garden and come back.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may not share your sensory profile. What they need to know: sensory overload in ADHD is not being dramatic. It is a real processing limit. When the parent says they are full, they are full. Pushing past it leads to a snap, not adaptation.`,
    moreHelp: `If sensory issues are significantly affecting daily life for you or your child, an occupational therapist with sensory integration training can provide specific strategies and environmental modifications. This is especially valuable for children whose sensory needs are creating daily conflicts around food, clothing, or hygiene.`,
    relatedCards: ["m32", "m33", "m29", "m30", "m31", "p30", "k03", "k04", "k05"],
  },

  {
    id: "g09",
    title: `Transitions and the dopamine gap`,
    topic: "transitions",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "juggler"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `Leaving the park is a meltdown. Turning off the screen is a war. Changing from one activity to another, even a good one, produces a reaction that seems wildly disproportionate.`,
    brain: `Transitions require executive function: stopping the current activity, mentally shifting to the next, initiating the new behaviour. Every step in that sequence uses the prefrontal cortex, which is the exact region ADHD impairs.

But there is a deeper mechanism. When an ADHD brain is engaged in a preferred activity, it is receiving a reliable dopamine supply. The game, the show, the play, these provide predictable, controllable, high-stimulation input. Stopping means losing that dopamine source. The transition is experienced as a neurological loss, not just a change of plans.

This is why even good transitions are hard. Going from home to the fun party still requires stopping what you are doing, which requires giving up the current dopamine source before the next one is available.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child is not being ungrateful for the fun you just provided. They are experiencing dopamine withdrawal. The meltdown at the park gate is the same mechanism as the rage when the screen turns off. Their brain had a reliable input and now it is gone.

Warnings help because they give the brain time to begin the internal transition. Two minutes, one minute, time is up. Each cue starts the process of neurologically letting go.`,
    dualDynamic: `If the parent also struggles with transitions, the parent's frustration at the child's transition difficulty may itself be a transition failure. You transitioned internally to 'time to leave' and your child has not. Your nervous system is now in conflict between the next plan and the current reality.`,
    strategies: [`Two-minute warning, one-minute warning, then time. Every time. Consistency in the warning structure makes the transition predictable.`, `Give them something to move toward, not just something being taken away. 'When we get in the car, you choose the music.'`, `Transition objects bridge the gap. A snack for the car, a game for the walk, a toy from home.`, `For screen shutoffs: a timer visible on the screen itself. The child sees time ending rather than experiencing a sudden loss.`, `Accept that transitions will take longer than you want. Build the time into the plan rather than fighting it as a delay.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `Before leaving somewhere fun`, say: `Two more minutes. When we leave, you get to pick what we listen to in the car.` },
      { situation: `When the screen needs to go off`, say: `The timer shows one minute. Save your game. When it is off, we are having a snack.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may see the transition meltdown as a tantrum. What they need to know: the child is experiencing a genuine neurological loss, not testing boundaries. The meltdown is withdrawal, not manipulation. Transition strategies that feel like 'giving in' are actually the most efficient way to move through it.`,
    moreHelp: `If every transition produces a significant meltdown, if your child cannot shift between activities without escalation, or if transitions are consuming most of your parenting energy, an occupational therapist or ADHD coach can help build transition routines tailored to your child's specific profile.`,
    relatedCards: ["m25", "m37", "m41", "m24", "m28", "m04", "m11"],
  },

  {
    id: "g10",
    title: `Sleep and the racing mind`,
    topic: "sleep",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "escaper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `It is 10pm and they are wired. Or it is 11pm and you are scrolling, unable to stop even though you are exhausted. The brain will not switch off for either of you.`,
    brain: `ADHD sleep problems affect 25 to 50 percent of people with ADHD. Three separate mechanisms are at work.

First, delayed sleep phase. The ADHD circadian rhythm runs later than neurotypical. Melatonin releases later. The body is genuinely not ready for sleep at the socially expected time.

Second, the default mode network. When external stimulation stops, the ADHD brain does not go quiet. It goes busy. Thoughts, worries, replays, plans, ideas all accelerate in the absence of something to focus on. A dark, quiet room is not calming for an ADHD brain. It is activating.

Third, stimulant medication can delay sleep onset further, depending on timing and individual response.

The result: a child lying in bed with their eyes open and their brain running, and a parent downstairs scrolling their phone for the same reason, unable to stop the input because stopping means facing the racing mind.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child lying awake is not being defiant. Their brain will not stop. The racing thoughts are involuntary. Telling them to 'just close your eyes and relax' is asking them to do something their neurology will not allow. They need something for the brain to follow, an audiobook, white noise, a guided relaxation, that gives the default mode network a single channel to track instead of generating its own.`,
    dualDynamic: `Both parent and child may be awake late for the same neurological reason. The parent scrolls because stopping the phone means facing the racing mind. The child cannot sleep because stopping the day means facing the same thing. Both brains need a transition from stimulation to rest that does not involve going from full input to zero input.`,
    strategies: [`Consistent wake time, even on weekends, matters more than bedtime. The circadian rhythm anchors to wake time.`, `Audiobook on a sleep timer gives the brain one channel to follow instead of generating racing thoughts.`, `Screen curfew 30 to 60 minutes before bed. The light and stimulation delay melatonin further. This applies to the parent too.`, `Talk to the prescriber about melatonin timing. Low-dose melatonin 30 to 60 minutes before target sleep time can shift the circadian rhythm.`, `If stimulant medication is contributing to sleep difficulty, the prescriber may adjust timing, switch formulations, or add a sleep-specific intervention.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When they say they cannot sleep`, say: `I know your brain is busy. Let us put a story on and you just listen. You do not have to try to sleep.` },
      { situation: `When you are doom-scrolling at midnight`, say: `I am doing the same thing they do. My brain does not want to stop. Phone down. Audiobook on.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may not understand why bedtime takes so long or why you are still awake at midnight. What they need to know: this is not a discipline issue or a phone addiction issue. It is a neurological sleep-onset problem that both parent and child share. The fix is environmental and sometimes pharmacological, not willpower.`,
    moreHelp: `If sleep problems are severe, if the child is regularly getting less than the recommended hours, or if daytime functioning is significantly impaired by poor sleep, see the prescriber. Sleep is one of the most modifiable ADHD symptoms with the right combination of behavioural strategies, melatonin timing, and medication adjustment.`,
    relatedCards: ["m38", "m04", "p22", "k13", "m42"],
  },

  {
    id: "g11",
    title: `The repair after rupture`,
    topic: "repair",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller", "reactor"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `Something happened. You yelled, snapped, said the wrong thing, or checked out when they needed you. Now there is a gap between you and your child that needs closing. The repair feels bigger than it needs to be.`,
    brain: `The repair is the most important parenting skill for ADHD families, because ruptures happen more often. The ADHD parent has a shorter fuse, a more impulsive tongue, and a regulatory system that fails more frequently. This means more repairs are needed, which means getting good at repair is not optional. It is a core skill.

The good news: research consistently shows that children's outcomes are determined not by whether ruptures happen, but by whether repairs follow. Rupture without repair damages the relationship. Rupture followed by consistent repair strengthens it, because the child learns that relationships can survive conflict.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child needs to know two things after a rupture. First, that what happened was not their fault (even if their behaviour contributed to the situation). Second, that the relationship is intact. They get both of these from a brief, calm repair and a return to normal interaction. They do not get them from an extended apology, an explanation, or a parent who is visibly still upset.`,
    dualDynamic: `In a dual-ADHD household, both parent and child may need to repair. The parent snapped. The child escalated. Both contributed. The parent goes first because they are the adult. The child's repair, if needed, can be gently invited later.`,
    strategies: [`The two-sentence repair: one sentence naming what happened, one naming what you will try. Then return to normal.`, `Timing matters. Do not repair while either of you is still activated. Wait until both nervous systems are back at baseline. This usually takes at least 20 minutes.`, `Do not ask the child to reassure you. 'Are you okay? Do you forgive me? Are we okay?' puts the emotional labour on the child. Make the repair and move on.`, `A physical gesture can be part of the repair: sitting next to them, getting them a snack, helping with something small. The return to normal IS the repair.`, `If you over-apologise or flood them with warmth, you are asking them to manage your guilt. Keep it brief. Keep it calm.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `After yelling`, say: `I yelled and I should not have. That is on me, not you.` },
      { situation: `After shutting down`, say: `I went quiet when you needed me. I am back now.` },
      { situation: `After saying something hurtful`, say: `I said something I did not mean. That was not true and I am sorry.` },
      { situation: `The simple return`, say: `Want a snack? Let us sit together for a bit.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may want to discuss the rupture at length. What they need to know: the repair with the child needs to be brief and calm. The processing of what happened can happen between adults, later, privately. The child does not need to witness the adults debriefing the incident.`,
    moreHelp: `If repairs are not happening, if the ruptures are getting more intense, or if your child is showing signs of being afraid of you, seek professional help. A family therapist who understands ADHD can teach repair skills and address patterns that are beyond self-management.`,
    relatedCards: ["p01", "p03", "m15", "p11", "m07", "p06", "p09"],
  },

  {
    id: "g12",
    title: `Your partner does not see what you see`,
    topic: "relationships",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller", "reactor", "looper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "partner"],
    recognize: `They see the reaction but not the thirty minutes of load that preceded it. They think the child is fine because the child is fine at the moment they are looking. They parent differently and the gap between your approaches is straining the relationship.`,
    brain: `ADHD is invisible. The internal experience of overwhelm, sensory overload, executive function failure, and emotional dysregulation is not visible to someone standing next to you. Your partner sees the output (the snap, the forgotten form, the caved boundary) but not the process that led to it.

If your partner does not have ADHD, they may genuinely not understand why you cannot 'just' stay calm, be consistent, or remember the form. These are easy tasks for their brain. The empathy gap is not malice. It is the fundamental difficulty of understanding a neurological difference you do not share.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child may behave differently with each parent, which can make the ADHD-aware parent feel like they are the problem. If the child is calmer with the other parent, it may be because the other parent provides a different regulatory environment, not because you are doing it wrong.`,
    dualDynamic: `The most common co-parenting split in ADHD families: one parent is the structure provider (usually the one who sees the ADHD more clearly) and one is the flexibility provider. The structure parent feels unsupported. The flexibility parent feels criticised. The child learns to play the gap.

The conversation is not 'who is right.' It is 'what does this child need from us as a unit.' This conversation works best with a therapist or coach present, because the emotional stakes are too high for most couples to navigate alone.`,
    strategies: [`Share one article or guide (like this one) that explains the specific mechanism you are trying to describe. It is easier for them to hear it from a third source than from you in an argument.`, `Choose one parenting approach to align on this week. Not everything. One thing. Present a united front on that one thing.`, `Divide and specialise. If one parent is better at mornings and one is better at bedtime, play to strengths rather than trying to be interchangeable.`, `Ask for the specific help you need, not general support. 'Can you handle bath time tonight so I can have 20 minutes alone' is actionable. 'I need more support' is not.`, `Do not discuss parenting disagreements in front of the child. The child will exploit the gap, not because they are manipulative, but because their ADHD brain naturally seeks the path of least resistance.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When they question your approach`, say: `I know it looks like I am giving in. What I am actually doing is choosing the battle that matters and letting this one go.` },
      { situation: `When you need them to read something`, say: `This explains the thing I have been trying to describe. Can you read it when you have ten minutes?` },
      { situation: `When you need a break`, say: `I need you to take over for 20 minutes. Not because I am failing. Because I have used up my capacity for today.` },
    ],
    partner: `This section IS for them. If you are the partner reading this: the parent with ADHD is not choosing to be inconsistent, reactive, or forgetful. They are managing a neurological difference that makes the standard parenting toolkit less reliable. The most helpful things you can do: believe them when they describe their internal experience, take over without commentary when they signal they are depleted, and discuss parenting disagreements privately rather than in the moment.`,
    moreHelp: `If the co-parenting relationship is significantly strained, couples therapy with an ADHD-aware therapist can be transformative. The key is finding someone who understands that the ADHD is a variable in the relationship, not an excuse.`,
    relatedCards: ["p04", "p05", "p06", "p40", "p27", "p28"],
  },

  {
    id: "g13",
    title: `Your child's social world`,
    topic: "social",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller"],
    perspectives: ["kid"],
    recognize: `They are eating lunch alone. The party invitations have stopped. They said something that pushed a friend away and they do not understand what they did wrong. Your heart is breaking for something you cannot directly fix.`,
    brain: `ADHD social processing is slower than neurotypical. Your child misses subtle social cues, misreads tone, interrupts, dominates play, or reacts too intensely to small social slights. The social operating system is running on different timing than their peers.

Verbal impulsivity means thoughts become words before the social filter can assess them. They say the wrong thing at the wrong time not because they are rude but because the gap between thinking and speaking is too short.

Rejection sensitivity means each social failure lands harder than it would for a neurotypical child. The accumulated weight of friendship losses builds a conviction that they are fundamentally unlikeable.`,
    kidBrain: `They want friends desperately. They do not understand why friendships keep failing. The social skills they lack are processing speed and inhibition, both ADHD impairments, not social knowledge. They usually know the rules. They cannot implement them fast enough.

One good friendship matters more than popularity. Structured social activities with clear rules are easier than unstructured play. A board game is easier than free play because the rules provide the social scaffolding their brain needs.`,
    dualDynamic: `If you also have ADHD, you may have your own social wounds from childhood. Watching your child experience the same exclusion can activate your own rejection sensitivity. Be aware that your reaction to their social pain may be amplified by your own history.`,
    strategies: [`Social skills coaching or groups, if accessible, build the specific skills that ADHD impairs. Parental instruction is less effective because the gap is processing speed, not knowledge.`, `Facilitate one-on-one playdates rather than group settings. One friend at a time is more manageable and more likely to succeed.`, `Do not say 'just be yourself.' Their natural self includes impulsivity that causes social friction. Help them build specific strategies for specific situations.`, `After a social difficulty, debrief privately and gently: 'What happened right before they got upset?' This builds social awareness without blame.`, `Regularly validate that they are likeable and worth knowing. They need to hear it from you because they are not hearing it from peers.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When they say nobody likes them`, say: `That sounds really lonely. Tell me about the person you liked talking to most recently.` },
      { situation: `After a friendship rupture`, say: `What happened right before they got upset? Let us think about what you could try next time.` },
      { situation: `When they blurted something inappropriate`, say: `I know that came out before you could catch it. Next time, try holding the thought for three seconds. I will practice with you.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may want to solve it by arranging more social events. What they need to know: quantity of social exposure without quality of social skills support can make things worse. The child needs scaffolding, not just opportunities.`,
    moreHelp: `If social isolation is severe, if your child is being bullied, or if they are refusing to attend school or social events because of social anxiety, a child psychologist can assess whether additional support is needed. Social skills groups specifically for ADHD and neurodivergent children are significantly more effective than general social skills programmes.`,
    relatedCards: ["k14", "k09", "k16", "k15", "k11", "m45"],
  },

  {
    id: "g14",
    title: `I think I have ADHD too`,
    topic: "identity",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller", "juggler"],
    perspectives: ["me"],
    recognize: `You were reading about your child's brain and you saw yourself in every paragraph. The disorganisation, the emotional intensity, the forgotten promises, the feeling of always running but never catching up. It has a name. It has always had a name. Nobody told you.`,
    brain: `Parent-recognised ADHD after child diagnosis is one of the most common adult ADHD discovery pathways. The pattern recognition is strong because you share the neurology. What you are seeing is real.

The realisation arrives in stages. First, recognition: 'that is me.' Then reframing: every 'lazy,' 'careless,' 'not trying hard enough' from your history gets a new explanation. Then grief: the years of misunderstanding, the relationships that might have been different, the career that might have gone another way. Then, eventually, something like relief: there is a reason. It was never a character flaw.`,
    kidBrain: `Your recognition changes what you can offer your child. You understand their brain from the inside, not just from observation. You know what the classroom felt like, what the social rejection felt like, what the 'why can't you just' felt like. This understanding is a gift, even though it comes with pain.`,
    dualDynamic: `Knowing you share the neurology changes the parenting dynamic. You can say 'my brain does that too' and mean it. You can separate the behaviour from the person because you know the mechanism from inside. But it also means you are parenting with the same impairments you are trying to help your child manage.`,
    strategies: [`Give yourself time with the recognition. You do not need to diagnose, disclose, or decide anything immediately.`, `Consider seeking formal assessment. Understanding your specific ADHD profile helps you get the right support, including medication if appropriate.`, `Separate the grief from the parenting. Your grief about your own past is valid but it is yours to process, not your child's to carry.`, `Connect with other adults who discovered ADHD later in life. The shared recognition is powerful and reduces isolation.`, `Use your lived experience as a parenting asset. You understand things about your child that no textbook can teach.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When the recognition hits`, say: `I think my brain works the same way. I am going to find out more.` },
      { situation: `When talking to your child about it`, say: `My brain works a lot like yours. That is why I understand some of what you are going through.` },
      { situation: `When the grief arrives`, say: `I am allowed to feel sad about what I did not know. And I can make sure my child does not wait as long as I did.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may not understand why a child's diagnosis has rocked you so deeply. What they need to know: you are not just processing your child's diagnosis. You are reprocessing your entire life through a new lens. This takes time and possibly professional support.`,
    moreHelp: `Seek formal assessment if the recognition is strong. An ADHD-aware psychiatrist or psychologist can provide diagnostic clarity. If the grief is significant, a therapist who understands late-diagnosed ADHD can help you process the reframing without it overwhelming your current life.`,
    relatedCards: ["p25", "p26", "p20", "m17", "p02"],
  },

  {
    id: "g15",
    title: `Burnout and being touched out`,
    topic: "burnout",
    patternTypes: ["escaper", "spiraller"],
    perspectives: ["me"],
    recognize: `You counted down to school drop-off and then drowned in guilt for the relief. You flinched when they hugged you because your body had nothing left to receive. You stopped accepting invitations because outings are too unpredictable. You are running on less than empty.`,
    brain: `Caregiver burnout in ADHD parents is not generic tiredness. It is the depletion of the specific neurological resources that ADHD already provides less of. Regulatory capacity, executive function, sensory processing bandwidth, emotional resilience, these are all finite and depletable, and ADHD means you start with less.

Being touched out is a real sensory state. After sustained physical contact, auditory input, and emotional demand, the ADHD sensory system reaches saturation. Every additional touch, question, or demand lands on a system that is already overloaded. This is not rejection of your child. It is a physiological limit.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child may sense your depletion without understanding it. They may escalate to try to get the version of you that has energy and warmth. This escalation increases your depletion, which increases their escalation. The cycle needs an intervention, and the intervention is you getting a break, not trying harder.`,
    dualDynamic: `The parent who needs rest and the child who needs connection are in direct conflict. Both needs are valid. Both are urgent. The solution is not powering through. It is brief, planned breaks that prevent the total depletion that makes everything worse.`,
    strategies: [`Relief is not betrayal. Being glad your child is at school means your nervous system needs recovery. Let the guilt go.`, `Name it without guilt: 'My body needs five minutes without being touched. I love you and I will be back.'`, `One social connection per week, even just a text. Isolation compounds burnout.`, `Identify the single most depleting daily task and find a way to change it. Automate it, delegate it, or drop it.`, `Stop comparing your capacity to other parents. Your operating system is running harder software. The fatigue is proportionate.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When you need space`, say: `I love you and my body needs five minutes of quiet. I am coming back.` },
      { situation: `When the guilt arrives`, say: `Needing a break does not mean I love them less. It means I have been giving everything I have.` },
      { situation: `When asked how you are`, say: `Honestly, I am running on empty. I need help this week.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may not see the depletion because you are still functioning. What they need to know: functioning while depleted is what ADHD parents do. It does not mean there is capacity left. The most helpful thing: take one thing off their plate this week without being asked.`,
    moreHelp: `If the burnout is affecting your ability to parent safely, if you are having thoughts of harming yourself, or if the depletion is sustained over weeks rather than days, talk to your GP. Parental burnout is a recognised condition that responds to intervention. You are not failing. You are depleted.`,
    relatedCards: ["p29", "p30", "p39", "p41", "p35", "p34", "p10"],
  },

  {
    id: "g16",
    title: `Extended family and judgment`,
    topic: "relationships",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller", "reactor"],
    perspectives: ["me", "partner"],
    recognize: `A grandparent said your child just needs more discipline. A relative implied you are too soft, too permissive, or that ADHD is not real. The comment cut deep because it hit the part of you that already wonders if they are right.`,
    brain: `ADHD rejection sensitivity means the relative's words activated pain circuits, not just disagreement. The comment was uninformed. The pain is real.

Extended family who do not understand ADHD often default to the parenting paradigm they know: compliance through authority. This worked, or seemed to work, in a generation that did not identify ADHD and interpreted its symptoms as defiance, laziness, or poor parenting. Their framework is not malicious. It is outdated.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child may overhear the criticism and internalise it. If grandma thinks they are naughty, that becomes data about who they are. Protecting your child from uninformed family commentary is not overprotecting. It is harm reduction.`,
    dualDynamic: `If you also have ADHD, the family member may have said similar things about you as a child. The comment about your parenting may activate memories of your own childhood criticism, doubling the pain.`,
    strategies: [`You do not owe them a defence of ADHD in the moment. A boundary is enough: 'We are managing this with professional support.'`, `Decide in advance what information to share and what to protect. Not everyone needs the full picture.`, `If a relative consistently undermines your parenting in front of your child, limit the exposure. Your child's self-concept matters more than a relative's comfort.`, `Share one simple resource if they are genuinely open to learning. If they are not, stop trying to convince them.`, `Process the hurt with someone who understands, not with the relative who caused it.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When they say it is bad parenting`, say: `I appreciate the concern. We are working with professionals on this.` },
      { situation: `When they undermine you in front of your child`, say: `We are going to handle this our way. Let us talk about it later, without the kids.` },
      { situation: `To your child after`, say: `Grandma sees things differently. What I know about your brain is that it works really hard and sometimes needs different help.` },
    ],
    partner: `If it is their family, they need to be the one who draws the boundary. You should not have to defend your parenting to your partner's relatives alone.`,
    moreHelp: `If family relationships are causing significant distress, a family therapist can help navigate the boundary-setting. Some families respond to education. Others respond only to limits.`,
    relatedCards: ["p27", "p28", "p07", "p32"],
  },

  {
    id: "g17",
    title: `When to get more help`,
    topic: "support",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "spiraller", "escaper", "juggler", "looper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `The pattern keeps happening. The cards help in the moment but the moments keep coming. You are wondering if you need more than self-help. You might be right.`,
    brain: `Self-awareness and self-management have limits. If the same pattern plays out weekly despite your best efforts, the issue may be structural: unoptimised medication, unaddressed co-occurring conditions, or a family dynamic that needs professional intervention.

Seeking help is not failure. It is the executive function decision to outsource a problem to someone with better tools. This is the most ADHD-appropriate response possible.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child may also need more support than you can provide at home. If their behaviour is escalating, if school is consistently difficult, if friendships are not forming, or if they are expressing self-harm or despair, professional assessment is warranted.`,
    dualDynamic: `In dual-ADHD families, the threshold for seeking help should be lower, not higher. Two impaired regulatory systems managing each other is harder than one. Professional support is not a luxury. It is a structural need.`,
    strategies: [`Signs you need help for yourself: daily loss of control, increasing intensity, avoidance of your child, persistent shame spiral, partner relationship deteriorating, physical symptoms of burnout.`, `Signs your child needs help: school refusal, self-harm talk, persistent friendlessness, escalating aggression, significant anxiety, regression in previously acquired skills.`, `Start with your GP. They can refer to ADHD-aware psychiatrists, psychologists, and family therapists.`, `ADHD coaching is for practical scaffolding: systems, routines, accountability. Therapy is for emotional processing: shame, grief, relationship repair. Both are valuable. They do different things.`, `Medication review: if current medication is not covering the parenting hours, if side effects are problematic, or if you are unmedicated and struggling, talk to the prescriber.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `Deciding to seek help`, say: `This is not something I can manage alone anymore. I am going to ask for support.` },
      { situation: `Calling the GP`, say: `I am an ADHD parent with an ADHD child and we are struggling. I need a referral to someone who understands both.` },
      { situation: `To your child`, say: `We are going to get some extra help to make things easier for both of us. That is a strong thing to do, not a weak one.` },
    ],
    partner: `If you are the partner: when the ADHD parent says they need help, believe them. Do not suggest they try harder. Do not delay. Help them make the appointment. The ask for help is itself an executive function achievement.`,
    moreHelp: `This entire guide IS the 'more help' guide. The next step is making the call. Your GP, a helpline, a therapist directory. One call. Today if possible. This week if not.`,
    relatedCards: ["p01", "p41", "p33", "p39", "p35", "p25"],
  },

  {
    id: "g18",
    title: `Your child at school`,
    topic: "school",
    patternTypes: ["spiraller", "juggler"],
    perspectives: ["kid"],
    recognize: `The school's name on your phone makes your stomach drop. It is another problem, another meeting, another email about behaviour. You dread the contact because it is almost never good news.`,
    brain: `Parents of children with ADHD receive approximately three times more phone calls from school than other parents. They are 29 percent less likely to attend optional school events. The conditioning is Pavlovian: school contact has been paired with negative reports so many times that the association is now automatic.

Your ADHD rejection sensitivity amplifies every school concern into a judgment of your parenting. The teacher's observation about focus becomes 'your child is a problem.' The request for a meeting becomes 'you are failing.' The email about a playground incident becomes 'everyone can see you cannot control your child.'`,
    kidBrain: `School asks your child to sustain attention, suppress impulses, navigate social complexity, and manage transitions for six consecutive hours. For an ADHD brain, this is running a marathon daily in a body built for sprints. The child who masks all day pays for it at home.

School refusal is not laziness. It is their nervous system anticipating a day of unsustainable demand. The stomach ache is real. The anxiety is producing genuine physical symptoms.`,
    dualDynamic: `If you also have ADHD, school communications may trigger memories of your own school experience. The teacher's concern about your child may activate your own childhood wounds around academic failure, social exclusion, or being 'the difficult one.'`,
    strategies: [`Let the school email sit for ten minutes before opening it. It will still be there. Read it when you have capacity.`, `Build a relationship with one teacher or coordinator who understands ADHD. A single ally in the school changes everything.`, `Advocate specifically. Not 'my child needs help' but 'my child needs movement breaks every 30 minutes and preferential seating away from the door.'`, `Keep a file of what works. When strategies succeed at school, document them. This becomes your evidence base for future conversations.`, `Separate the school's assessment from your parenting worth. The school sees your child for six hours in one specific environment. You see the full picture.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When the school calls`, say: `Thank you for letting me know. What specifically would help right now?` },
      { situation: `When advocating for accommodation`, say: `Here is what works at home. Can we try something similar at school?` },
      { situation: `To your child about school`, say: `School is harder for your brain. That does not mean you cannot do it. It means it costs you more than other kids.` },
    ],
    partner: `If one parent handles all school communication, the load is unbalanced. Share the school contact. Alternate meetings. Both parents should be on the school's contact list.`,
    moreHelp: `If school refusal is persistent, if your child is being excluded or suspended, or if the school is not providing reasonable accommodations, seek advice from a disability advocacy service or education consultant. Your child has rights. The school has obligations.`,
    relatedCards: ["p36", "p42", "k08", "m14", "p07"],
  },

  {
    id: "g19",
    title: `Food, clothing, and the daily sensory battles`,
    topic: "sensory",
    patternTypes: ["reactor", "juggler"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `The sock seam. The food touching. The wrong texture. The tag that must be cut. The toothpaste that gags. Every morning and every mealtime is a negotiation with a nervous system that processes sensation differently.`,
    brain: `Sensory processing differences frequently co-occur with ADHD. Inputs that neurotypical brains filter out arrive at full intensity in your child's nervous system. The sock seam is not uncomfortable. It is unbearable. The food texture is not unpleasant. It triggers alarm.

For the ADHD parent who is also sensory-sensitive, the daily management of their child's sensory needs may itself be overwhelming. You are accommodating sensory demands while managing your own sensory overload.`,
    kidBrain: `Your child is not being difficult about clothes or food. Their nervous system is rejecting inputs that genuinely register as painful or threatening. Sensory aversions are neurological, not behavioural. Forcing tolerance does not build resilience. It builds negative associations and increases the meltdown probability.

The foods they will eat, the clothes they will wear, the textures they can tolerate, these are data about their sensory profile, not evidence of fussiness.`,
    dualDynamic: `The parent who needs a smooth morning and the child whose nervous system rejects the first three clothing options are in structural conflict. The solution is accommodation, not enforcement. Buy the seamless socks. Cut every tag. Find the five foods they will eat and stop fighting about the rest.`,
    strategies: [`Accommodate first, discuss later. Cut the tag now. Buy the seamless socks. Find the toothpaste that works. Every accommodation removes a daily conflict.`, `For food: one safe food on every plate. No commentary on what they did not eat. Repeated low-pressure exposure over weeks, not forced consumption tonight.`, `For clothing: let them have input. If they can only wear soft cotton, buy soft cotton. The goal is dressed, not fashion-compliant.`, `For hygiene: let them control the sensory details. Water temperature, toothpaste flavour, order of steps. Autonomy over sensory input reduces refusal.`, `Keep a sensory profile: what they tolerate, what they seek, what they avoid. This data helps occupational therapists and school staff provide appropriate support.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When the clothing battle starts`, say: `I can see that feels wrong on your body. Let us find something that feels okay.` },
      { situation: `When they refuse food`, say: `You do not have to eat that. Here is your safe food. The other one is there if you want to try it.` },
      { situation: `To yourself when frustrated`, say: `This is their nervous system, not their attitude. I can accommodate this.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may see the accommodation as giving in. What they need to know: sensory processing differences are neurological. Forcing a child to tolerate unbearable sensation does not build resilience. It builds anxiety and avoidance.`,
    moreHelp: `An occupational therapist with sensory integration training can assess your child's sensory profile and provide specific strategies. This is especially valuable if sensory issues are affecting multiple areas of daily life: eating, dressing, hygiene, sleep, and school participation.`,
    relatedCards: ["m29", "m30", "m31", "k03", "k04", "k05", "m32", "m33"],
  },

  {
    id: "g20",
    title: `The argument loop`,
    topic: "communication",
    patternTypes: ["looper"],
    perspectives: ["me", "kid"],
    recognize: `You are three explanations deep and they stopped listening two explanations ago. You can see it is not working and you cannot stop. The pull to explain, justify, and make them understand is stronger than the evidence that it is making things worse.`,
    brain: `ADHD brains have a strong justice sensitivity and a need for cognitive closure. When your child challenges a decision, your brain does not just hear a child being difficult. It hears an unresolved problem that requires a response.

The impulsivity that drives the engagement is neurological. Walking away from an unresolved argument with an ADHD brain feels almost physically uncomfortable, like leaving a sentence half-finished.

Your child, if they also have ADHD, has the same drive. Two people with ADHD who both need closure, arguing, will not find it together. The loop runs because both nervous systems are seeking a resolution that the format of the argument cannot produce.`,
    kidBrain: `When they are dysregulated, their brain can process tone and volume but not complex reasoning. Your explanation does not land as information. It lands as more input, more intensity, more of what they need to push back against. After the first ten seconds of a lecture, they are managing the experience of being lectured, not processing the content.`,
    dualDynamic: `Two ADHD brains in an argument loop will escalate rather than resolve. The parent needs to be right. The child needs to be heard. Neither can disengage because both need closure. One of you has to stop first. It will not be the child.`,
    strategies: [`Learn one stop-script. Say it once. Then physically move. The movement is part of the practice. Your nervous system will interpret physical disengagement as a signal to stop seeking closure.`, `The lesson that would take the argument twenty minutes to fail to deliver can land in two minutes twelve hours later, when both of you are regulated.`, `Count your words. If you have said more than three sentences, you have said too much. One sentence is enough.`, `Notice the pull to justify. When you feel the need to explain why you are right, that is the signal to stop, not the signal to explain harder.`, `After the argument, do not revisit it for at least two hours. The emotional residue needs to clear before either brain can process what happened.`],
    scripts: [
      { situation: `When you feel the pull to explain`, say: `My answer is not changing. We are done talking about this.` },
      { situation: `When they push back`, say: `I have said what I needed to say. We can talk about it tomorrow.` },
      { situation: `Mid-argument when you catch yourself`, say: `I am going to stop here. I have said enough.` },
    ],
    partner: `They may see you engaging in a debate with a seven-year-old about screen time limits and wonder why you cannot just use your authority. What they need to understand: the pull is neurological. Walking away from an unresolved argument is physically uncomfortable for an ADHD brain. It is not stubbornness. It is how the brain is wired.`,
    moreHelp: `If the argument loops are happening daily, if they are escalating to shouting or physical intensity, or if your child is developing adversarial patterns with you specifically, family therapy can help rebuild the communication pattern. The therapist can teach both of you to recognise the loop and exit it before it runs.`,
    relatedCards: ["m10", "m16", "m08", "p08", "m17", "m11"],
  },
];

